I think that "no" is as close an estimate as I can confidently give ;-)
There are a whole lot of "probably not"s involved, which multiply down
to "almost certainly not":

The Windows build you are using probably uses the internal compression
routine on the save files, whereas the linux version will likely be
using gzip instead.

Last time I looked at a nethack save file (on linux), one of the things
stored was the numerical user ID - I don't know if the Windows version
does such a thing, but if it did, it would be some coincidence that you
happened to have the same numerical user ID on both machines.

But both of the above are trivial compared to the next:  The main way in
which the save files aren't especially portable is that they contain
huge dumps of binary data, and, from my limited understanding of the C
standard, the data types used (and the possible padding between them)
aren't required to occupy the same amount of space on different
platforms.  Not even if both platforms are using the same processor.

